  "question": {
    "text": "The figure shows an elliptical path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> of a planet around the sun <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> such that the area of triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></math> is <math><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> the area of the ellipse. (see figure) with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></math> as the major axis, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></math> as the minor axis. If <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is the time taken for the planet to go over path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> for path taken over&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></math> then:<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0174_24108bb9afbc.png\" style=\"width: 327px; height: 177px;\" />"
